Model Metrics in FedMed

Model Metrics means adding a performance-evaluation module to FedMed that measures how accurately the machine-learning model predicts medical outcomes.

1. Purpose

The Model Metrics module helps determine whether the trained FedMed model is performing properly. Instead of showing only the prediction, the system can provide quantitative measures of model performance.

2. Main Metrics
Metric	Meaning
Accuracy	Percentage of total predictions that are correct.
Precision	How many predicted positive cases are actually positive.
Recall	How many actual positive cases the model successfully identifies.
F1-Score	Balance between precision and recall.
Confusion Matrix	Shows correct and incorrect predictions for each class.
ROC-AUC	Measures how well the model distinguishes between different classes.
3. Example

Suppose FedMed predicts whether a patient belongs to a particular medical risk class.

If the test dataset contains 100 patients and the model correctly predicts 92 patients, then:

Accuracy = 92%

But accuracy alone may not be sufficient for medical prediction. For example, if identifying high-risk patients is important, Recall becomes particularly useful because it indicates how many actual high-risk cases were detected.

4. How it works in FedMed

The workflow can be:

Patient Dataset
      ↓
Data Preprocessing
      ↓
Trained ML Model
      ↓
Test Dataset
      ↓
Generate Predictions
      ↓
Compare Actual vs Predicted
      ↓
Calculate Metrics
      ↓
Display Results